1. Explain what kind of behavior you are getting and how you think it should work.

When altering a table requires the table to be recreated, all columns are copied into the new table, causing an error if there are any generated columns, as they can be read, but not inserted. Instead of passing no columns to copyData and defaulting to `*`, the names of all non-generated columns should be explicitly defined.

Example of a change that could fix this:
```js
const copyColumnNames = parsedTable.columns.filter(c => !c.constraints.as?.generated).map(c => c.name)
return this.generateAlterCommands(newTable, createIndices, copyColumnNames);
```

2. Error message

For a table that has a generated column, you'll get an error similar to this:
`migration failed with error: INSERT INTO "_knex_temp_alter244" SELECT * FROM "test"; - SQLITE_ERROR: table _knex_temp_alter244 has 2 columns but 3 values were supplied`


3. Reduced test code, for example in https://npm.runkit.com/knex or if it needs real
